    <h2>What imgready does</h2>
    <p>Compress, convert and resize images with the same WebAssembly encoders professional tools use — WebP, AVIF, JPG, PNG and GIF on the way out; HEIC, HEIF, TIFF, BMP, SVG on the way in. Batch any number of files. Output multiple formats in a single pass. Drop a folder. Strip EXIF. Use a platform preset. Live-edit quality with the fullscreen compare editor.</p>
    <p>Encoding runs in your browser. The <a href="/why/">thinking behind it</a> goes deeper into what that means for speed and file-size limits.</p>

    <h3>Supported formats</h3>
    <table class="format-table"><tr><th>Format</th><th>Best for</th><th>Compression</th></tr><tr><td><strong>WebP</strong></td><td>Modern web — blogs, e-commerce</td><td>25–35% smaller than JPEG</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AVIF</strong></td><td>Maximum compression, photos</td><td>Up to 50% smaller than JPEG</td></tr><tr><td><strong>JPEG / JPG</strong></td><td>Universal compatibility</td><td>Lossy, works everywhere</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PNG</strong></td><td>Screenshots, graphics, transparency</td><td>Lossless at 100, lossy PNG-8 below</td></tr><tr><td><strong>GIF</strong></td><td>Simple animations, flat colour</td><td>Limited to 256 colours</td></tr></table>

    <h3>Common use cases</h3>
    <p>imgready is designed for the everyday image work that doesn't deserve a separate app. A few of the things it gets used for:</p>
    <ul class="use-cases">
      <li><strong>iPhone photos for Windows colleagues.</strong> Drop a HEIC, get a JPG back. No app install, no email back-and-forth about &quot;why won't this open&quot;.</li>
      <li><strong>Hero images for blog posts.</strong> Resize a 5&nbsp;MB phone photo to 1600&nbsp;px wide WebP under 200&nbsp;KB. Pages load faster, Lighthouse score climbs, no CDN cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Shopify or Etsy product photography.</strong> Drop a folder of 50 photos, set a platform preset, hit Process All, download a single ZIP. Aspect ratios stay correct.</li>
      <li><strong>EXIF stripping before sharing.</strong> Phone photos carry GPS coordinates by default. imgready strips them invisibly before you post — no extra step, no extra tool.</li>
    </ul>

